      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Widespread ST-segment depression with inverted T waves maximally in leads V4-V5 (ie, the global ischemia electrocardiogram [ECG] pattern) is a marker of adverse outcome in patients with non-ST-segment elevation acute coronary syndrome (ACS), perhaps because this pattern is indicative of left main stem stenosis. However, the prognostic value of this ECG pattern has not yet been established."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We studied the predictive value of a prespecified ECG pattern in patients who underwent urgent or emergent coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We studied the sensitivity, specificity, and predictive values for the global ischemia ECG to predict angiographic left main coronary artery disease. Patients with a 12-lead ECG recorded during anginal symptoms before CABG were included."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The global ischemia ECG pattern was found in 61 (76%) of 80 patients with and 12 (19%) of 65 patients without left main disease. The sensitivity, specificity, and positive and negative predictive values for left main coronary artery disease in patients with the global ischemia ECG pattern were 76%, 81%, 84%, and 74%, respectively. In multivariate analysis, the global ischemia ECG pattern was strongly associated with angiographic left main coronary artery disease after adjusting for age, gender, diabetes, hypertension, and smoking (hazard ratio, 16.0; 95% confidence interval, 6.5-39.5; P < 0.001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The global ischemia ECG pattern was strongly associated with angiographic left main coronary artery disease in patients who underwent urgent or emergent CABG."
        }
      ]},
